  WHY THE BUILD RAN IN A PRIVATE CLONE OF THE PIN RATHER THAN IN SPARK_SRC.

  AAP 0.5.1 requires the pinned tree be cloned BY SHA rather than by branch name,
  into a directory outside the working checkout. Two facts decide which directory:

    - /opt/spark-src is shared read-only across up to 45 concurrent clones of this
      job, and a sibling is probably mid-scan against it and its build output. A
      full-reactor 'package' there would rewrite artifacts under the feet of a
      running scan. harness/artifacts/logs/maven-preflight.log holds itself to the
      same rule in its own words: 'It did not write to the pinned clone. That tree
      is shared read-only across concurrent clones and a sibling may be mid-scan
      against it.'
    - the build already present in /opt/spark-src is NOT this build. It was
      NARROWED with -pl: harness/ENVIRONMENT.md section 5 quotes its command
      verbatim, with '-pl core,common/network-common,...,resource-managers/yarn -am
      package', and records a reactor of 33 projects and 32 of 32 JAR projects. Its
      seven absent build directories - tools, examples, assembly and the four
      connector/kafka-0-10* modules - are measured at STEP 3 and again at STEP 15.
      A narrowed build cannot answer a question about the full reactor.

  So the tree was cloned again, by SHA, into this clone's own scratch directory,
  and built there. The clone is not a copy of /opt/spark-src's object store: it was
  fetched anonymously from the upstream repository at the SHA (STEP 4). That it is
  the same code is then established by reading files rather than by trusting either
  fetch: both trees carry 25,641 tracked files and the identical rolled-up digest
  a6844cc3d41d890789012fb77e7c160920bfa172eb1406681298d2c001178222 over every
  tracked file's content, so BUILD_TREE_MATCHES_SPARK_SRC=yes.

  Two consequences a later reader must carry, because they are the two ways this
  file and cpg-input-inventory.json could be misread as disagreeing:

    - PATHS in STEP 13 are relative to the build tree named above. The same
      relative path under /opt/spark-src names the same project's artifact where
      the narrowed build produced one, and names nothing where it did not.
    - DIGESTS in STEP 13 are of THIS build's artifacts. A jar entry carries a build
      timestamp, so a second build of identical source yields a different sha256.
      The cross-document check is therefore project -> outcome and artifact ->
      relative path. It is NOT a digest comparison, and a digest difference between
      two builds is not a discrepancy.

THE COMMAND

  The mandated form, which is the FULL reactor - explicitly NOT narrowed with -pl
  (AAP 0.5.1):

      ./build/mvn -DskipTests \
        -Pyarn -Pkubernetes -Phive -Phive-thriftserver -Pvolcano package

  The exact invocation, as run, with the two additions labelled. It appears again,
  character for character, in the runner script quoted at STEP 10 and its first
  output line opens PART 2:

      cd /tmp/blitzy-harness-scratch/17/build-reactor/spark-src
      export MAVEN_OPTS="-Xss64m -Xmx6g -Xms2g -XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize=512m"
      ./build/mvn --no-transfer-progress -DskipTests \
        -Dmaven.repo.local=/tmp/blitzy-harness-scratch/17/build-reactor/m2-repo \
        -Pyarn -Pkubernetes -Phive -Phive-thriftserver -Pvolcano package

  There is no -pl and no -am anywhere in it, and no module selector of any kind:
  the reactor is whole. The five profile flags are exactly the five mandated ones,
  in the mandated order. The two additions, each recorded rather than assumed
  harmless:

    --no-transfer-progress   suppresses per-artifact transfer chatter only. It is
                             the form harness/artifacts/logs/maven-preflight.log
                             names as what it authorises, and the form the
                             provisioning build used (ENVIRONMENT.md section 5). It
                             changes no goal, no profile and no module.
    -Dmaven.repo.local=...   points the build at a private local repository, a
                             byte-exact copy of the primed /root/.m2/repository
                             (STEP 9). The shared original is in the same
                             never-disturb set as /opt/spark-src, and a full
                             reactor resolves coordinates the narrowed build never
                             needed - there is no org/apache/kafka in the primed
                             repository at all - so building against the shared
                             path would have WRITTEN into it. STEP 15 re-reads the
                             shared repository: 867 jars and 5,832 files before and
                             after, and still no org/apache/kafka. The private copy
                             went from 867 jars / 5,832 files to 901 / 6,023, and
                             that growth is the record of what this build resolved.

  MAVEN_OPTS is the runbook's value, unchanged. Nothing was installed: the wrapper's
  own first line of output, 'Using `mvn` from path: /usr/local/bin/mvn', is the
  wrapper stating which mvn it took, and STEP 8 and STEP 15 show no
  apache-maven* distribution in the build tree's build/ directory before or after.

WHY EVERY FLAG IS LOAD-BEARING, AND WHY FIVE FLAGS ADD FOUR MODULES

  Read without this arithmetic, 'five flags, four modules' looks like a mistake. It
  is not: the five flags divide into three that expand the reactor and two that do
  not (AAP 0.5.1). Every locator below is measured at STEP 6, in the tree that was
  built.

  THREE ROOT PROFILES THAT ADD CHILD MODULES - four modules from three flags:

    -Pyarn              pom.xml:3384 - adds resource-managers/yarn AND
                        common/network-yarn. Two modules from one flag, which is
                        the whole of the arithmetic's apparent oddity.
    -Pkubernetes        pom.xml:3392 - adds resource-managers/kubernetes/core
    -Phive-thriftserver pom.xml:3407 - adds sql/hive-thriftserver

  TWO MODULE-LOCAL PROFILES THAT ADD NOTHING TO THE REACTOR AND CHANGE WHAT GETS
  COMPILED:

    -Phive     There is NO root profile with the id 'hive' - STEP 6 greps for it in
               the root pom and does not find it; the root's hive-adjacent profile
               is 'hive-provided' at pom.xml:3520. sql/hive is listed
               unconditionally at pom.xml:98, so it needs no profile to enter the
               reactor. -Phive resolves inside sql/hive/pom.xml, at line 209, and
               dropping it changes what 'package' compiles there. Anyone who
               "corrects" the flag list by removing it is introducing a change.
    -Pvolcano  There is no root 'volcano' profile either. It resolves in the
               Kubernetes module: the profile is declared at
               resource-managers/kubernetes/core/pom.xml:36, and its build-helper
               execution 'add-volcano-source' at line 56 adds
               'volcano/src/main/scala' at line 63 as a source root. Those three
               line numbers are the PINNED tree's, measured at STEP 6 in the tree
               that was built. The same three constructs sit at :36, :59 and :66 in
               the working checkout's tip, and the tip's numbers are quoted in
               several places upstream of this file - the same one-line pin-vs-tip
               drift maven-preflight.log records for .gitignore's
               'build/apache-maven*' (line 38 at the pin, 39 at the tip). Both are
               true of their own tree; neither is a correction of the other, and
               this file reports what it measured.

               Without -Pvolcano the in-scope VolcanoFeatureStep is never compiled
               and never reaches the graph - and a graph query for it then returns
               nothing, WHICH READS EXACTLY LIKE A CLEAN RESULT (AAP 0.1.4). STEP 6
               confirms the source root exists in the built tree and that
               VolcanoFeatureStep.scala is inside it.

  Omit any of the three expanding flags and there is no bytecode for the YARN
  shuffle service, the Kubernetes resource manager or the Thrift server; omit
  either module-local one and a scoped source root goes uncompiled. In both cases
  the silence downstream is indistinguishable from a clean scan, which is why no
  flag is dropped even when the reactor would still build without it.

THE REACTOR THESE FLAGS PRODUCE: 40 MAVEN PROJECTS, 38 OF THEM PACKAGING A JAR

  Counted from the poms at STEP 6, before the build, and then checked against
  Maven's own Reactor Build Order at STEP 12:

      35   unconditional child modules            pom.xml:78-115, counted 35
    +  4   of the 11 profile-gated child modules  the three root profiles above
    -----
      39   child modules
    +  1   the root parent project itself         pom.xml:30 <packaging>pom</...>
    -----
      40   Maven projects in the reactor

  Of those 40, 38 package a JAR and 2 package none. The 2 are the root parent and
  assembly, and both are EXPECTED to produce no artifact - assembly is
  <packaging>pom</packaging> in its own pom, as STEP 6's per-module listing and
  Maven's own '[pom]' marker at STEP 12 both show. STEP 13 records for each of them
  'own artifact: NONE - EXPECTED, packaging=pom' rather than leaving the absence to
  be inferred. Two details there are worth stating so neither is misread:

    - the root parent's build directory does contain a
      spark-parent_2.13-4.1.0-SNAPSHOT-tests.jar. That is an attached test-jar
      artifact, not a main artifact; the parent still produces no main artifact,
      which is the expected outcome. STEP 13 lists it as 'also' rather than 'MAIN'.
    - assembly's build directory contains 340 jars, every one of them a copied
      runtime dependency written there by pom.xml's copy-module-dependencies, and
      not one of them assembly's own. STEP 13 counts and excludes them.

  python/pyspark is NOT a reactor entry and none is invented for it. It is one of
  the twelve authoritative scope roots and is scanned, but it is no Maven module:
  STEP 14 shows the root pom has no <module> entry for it and that it carries no
  pom.xml of its own. Its expected non-JAR outcome belongs to
  oss-scan-results/run-record.md, not to this file's reactor table. The same holds
  for resource-managers/kubernetes/docker, which the file-based tools reach through
  the mid-path ** of the kubernetes glob.

