  The expected-values table names 32 JAR producers, and it was measured over the
  NARROWED provisioning build - ENVIRONMENT.md section 6 tables exactly those 32
  across a 33-project reactor. A full reactor packages 38, so six are new to it:

      tools
      examples
      connector/kafka-0-10-token-provider
      connector/kafka-0-10
      connector/kafka-0-10-sql
      connector/kafka-0-10-assembly

  All six built and all six appear in Maven's reactor summary as SUCCESS; STEP 12
  isolates their six summary lines, and STEP 13 confirms each produced its own main
  artifact on disk. This is a RECORDED DIFFERENCE and not an error. The halt rule is
  one-directional (AAP 0.8.3): a module that produced a JAR in the rehearsal and
  produces none now is a halt; the reverse is not. The six legitimately enter the
  graph input set, and they are the reason the graph's method count is checked as a
  floor rather than a window - an anchor measured over 32 JAR producers cannot bound
  a graph built from 38.

  Nothing was trimmed in either direction to make a number fit. The reactor is the
  reactor the five mandated flags produce.

IF THE REACTOR HAD FAILED: THE DIAGNOSTIC PASS, AND ITS CONVENTIONS

  IT DID NOT FAIL. Maven exited 0, the summary carries 40 SUCCESS lines and no
  FAILURE or SKIPPED line, and every one of the 38 JAR-packaging projects was
  confirmed to have produced its own artifact. THE DIAGNOSTIC PASS WAS THEREFORE
  NOT NEEDED AND WAS NOT RUN, AND NO harness/artifacts/logs/build-<module>.log FILE
  EXISTS - not one, for any module, because there was nothing for it to establish.
  That absence is stated here rather than left silent: an absence stated is
  checkable, an absence unmentioned is not.

  The convention is recorded anyway, because this file is its only durable home
  (AAP 0.5.1) and a later run that does fail needs it:

    ORDER        Take the reactor build order Maven printed for the failed run -
                 PART 2 carries it, and it is the same order the summary follows -
                 and walk it front to back. That order is deterministic and
                 dependency-correct, so an upstream project is always attempted
                 before anything that needs it.
    INVOCATION   Per project, in that order:

                     ./build/mvn -DskipTests \
                       -Pyarn -Pkubernetes -Phive -Phive-thriftserver -Pvolcano \
                       -pl <module-path> -am package

    WHY -am IS   The reactor used 'package', not 'install', so no project's
    REQUIRED     SNAPSHOT siblings were written to the local repository. Without
                 -am a selected project would fail resolving its own upstream
                 SNAPSHOT dependencies - a failure about the local repository's
                 contents rather than about the project under test, which is
                 exactly the misattribution the pass exists to avoid.
    ATTRIBUTION  Maven names the failing project in its reactor summary. The
                 failure is attributed to THAT project, never to the one selected
                 by -pl. That distinction is what separates a project's own
                 compilation failure from an upstream dependency's. A project
                 already attributed an outcome is NOT re-attributed by a later -am
                 rebuild, so each project contributes exactly one entry and no
                 phantom project and no phantom input can appear.
    EVIDENCE     One log per invocation, retained verbatim, at
                 harness/artifacts/logs/build-<slug>.log, where <slug> is a
                 deterministic function of the module path: every '/' replaced by
                 '_' and nothing else changed. So sql/hive-thriftserver gives
                 build-sql_hive-thriftserver.log, resource-managers/kubernetes/core
                 gives build-resource-managers_kubernetes_core.log, and core gives
                 build-core.log.
    NO LICENCE   The pass is diagnostic and confers no licence to reduced coverage.
                 The run halts unless EVERY one of the 38 JAR-packaging projects
                 produced its own artifact, including the six the expected-values
                 list does not name, and the halt report names each that did not
                 and quotes its log (AAP 0.5.1, 0.9.2).

